From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: kernelbase: the time zone and the clock are set through sg-admind

Wine's time zone and clock are the host's, and setting either needs root:
SetTimeZoneInformation, SetDynamicTimeZoneInformation, SetSystemTime and
SetLocalTime always failed with ERROR_PRIVILEGE_NOT_HELD. On Stained Glass
an administrator's elevated program is the SYSTEM account, and sg-shell's
sg-admind is the root service that makes such changes for it: a request is
a file in its spool (/run/stained-glass-admin, which only SYSTEM can write),
its answer a file in the replies.

- SetDynamicTimeZoneInformation asks for "timezone <IANA zone>" for the
  zone its key names (a table from CLDR's windowsZones.xml, Unicode data
  under the Unicode License v3); an unknown key is ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER.
  SetTimeZoneInformation does the same when its standard name is a zone key
  (as Windows' zones' standard names are); anything else is refused as
  before.
- SetLocalTime / SetSystemTime (converted to local time) ask for "time
  YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS" when the change is not the few milliseconds ntdll
  allows itself.
- Without the service, or for a caller who cannot write the spool (a
  standard user): ERROR_PRIVILEGE_NOT_HELD, as on Windows. A refusal
  ("Set time automatically" is on) is ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED.
- SG_ADMIN_SPOOL names another spool, as for sg-shell's Control Panel (the
  gates'; the elevation broker passes no such variable).

Gate: test/tzset-gate.sh (the real sg-admind in its test mode).
---
